course: dictionary
lesson: 7208
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the sentence tells you which one to use. The word phase is a good example. In «The Sun always lights half of the Moon, like a lamp lighting half of a ball», phase is about the shapes the Moon seems to have. In «"Going through a phase" means behaving badly for a while», it is a short time when someone acts a certain way.
explain: Look at the words around the word. In science, the Moon's phases come from how much of its lit half we can see as it goes round us. Ask yourself: does this sentence talk about the sky, a person, or a plan? That question will often lead you to the right meaning.
explain: Some words are used in a different way with another small word. To phase something out means to stop using it slowly, step by step. Here the word out changes the meaning, so always read the whole phrase, not just the first word.
fact: A phase is one stage of something that changes over time.
fact: «The Moon makes no light of its own; it reflects sunlight, and its phases come from how much of its lit half we can see.»
fact: The Moon's phases go from new moon to full moon and back, about every 29.5 days.
fact: «Going through a phase» means behaving in a certain way for a short while.
fact: To phase something out means to stop using it slowly, step by step.
exercise: «The Sun always lights half of the Moon, and as the Moon goes round us we see more or less of that lit half, and these are the phases.» In this sentence, what are «these»? | Clouds that cover the sky | *The different shapes we see of the lit half | Stars that shine at night | Planets in the solar system
exercise: «"Going through a phase" means behaving badly for a while.» In this sentence, what does phase mean? | *A short time when someone behaves in a certain way | A type of rock found in the hills | A long holiday by the sea | A kind of loud noise
exercise: «Schools teach the sounds in a planned order, in stages called phases or sets.» Which word in this sentence means the same as stages? | order | sounds | planned | *phases
test: «Our calendar follows the sky: a day is one turn of the Earth, a month comes from the Moon's phases.» What does a month come from? | A school timetable | The Sun's heat | *The Moon's phases | The ocean tides
test: A shop will phase out paper bags. What will it do? | Sell every bag at once | Give them all away free today | Start giving out more paper bags | *Stop using them slowly, step by step
exam: «The Moon makes no light of its own; it reflects sunlight, and its phases come from how much of its lit half we can see.» What do the phases of the Moon come from? | Clouds covering the Moon | *How much of its lit half we can see | The Moon making its own light | The Moon being very hot
